"A cyclic flat embedding theorem for transversal $q$-matroids (https://arxiv.org/abs/2603.13550)\nciteturn0academia12 [wordlim: 200] Published: 7 months ago; Title: A cyclic flat embedding theorem for transversal $q$-matroids ... We prove that the class of transversal $q$-matroids is closed under the free product and propose a natural presentation for the direct sum motivated by the corresponding construction for matroids.\nTitle: A cyclic flat embedding theorem for transversal $q$-matroids\nAuthors: Andrew Fulcher\nDate: Fri Mar 13 19:39:06 2026\n\nCyclic flats form a common structural invariant of both matroids and $q$-matroids, determining these objects through their weighted lattices of cyclic flats. In this paper we exploit this perspective to establish a correspondence between matroids and a subclass of $q$-matroids that we call coordinate $q$-matroids. Our main result is a cyclic flat embedding theorem showing that the cyclic flat structure of a transversal matroid is preserved under this correspondence. This provides a mechanism for transferring structural properties from matroid theory to the $q$-matroid setting. As an application, we show that nested $q$-matroids are transversal and therefore representable. Finally, we illustrate the usefulness of this perspective by analysing transversal $q$-matroids under binary operations. We prove that the class of transversal $q$-matroids is closed under the free product and propose a natural presentation for the direct sum motivated by the corresponding construction for matroids.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nA cyclic flat embedding theorem for transversal $q$-matroids (https://www.researchgate.net/publication/402481075_A_cyclic_flat_embedding_theorem_for_transversal_q-matroids)\nciteturn0search0 [wordlim: 200] Published: 8 months ago; Crawled: 2 months ago; constructive proof that the class of transversal q-matroids is closed under the free product. ... In doing so, we propose a presentation for the direct sum of transversal q-matroids motivated ... The following conjecture provides a plausible example.\n\nthe q-analogue. In particular, it has been shown that the direct sum of representable q-matroids need not\n\nbe representable [10]. Moreover, it remains open whether the free product of representable q-matroids is\n\nrepresentable.\n\nUnderstanding when representability of q-matroids is preserved under binary operations is therefore an\n\nimportant problem. Such a characterisation would provide structural tools for constructing new vector rank-\n\nmetric codes. Some progress in this direction has recently been made in [3], which also highlights connections\n\nbetween q-matroid theory and ﬁnite geometry.\n\nOne of the rare instances in which a concept from matroid theory extends to q-matroid theory in a\n\nparticularly direct way is that of cyclic ﬂats. This has been explored in [1,2,9], where the theory of cyclic\n\nﬂats for q-matroids was developed and their behaviour under binary operations was studied.\n\n1\n\narXiv:2603.13550v1 [math.CO] 13 Mar 2026\n\nIn this paper we formalise a correspondence between matroids and a subclass of q-matroids that we call\nWe also show\n\nthat closure of the class of coordinate transversal q-matroids under direct sums follows from the cyclic ﬂat\n\nembedding theorem of the previous section. This yields a further class of representable q-matroids.\n\n2 Preliminaries\n\nWe assume that the reader has some familiarity with matroid theory [14] and q-matroid theory [11], for\n\nwhich we have provided references. q-matroids can be deﬁned in several cryptomorphic ways. In this paper,\n\nwe focus on deﬁnitions via the rank function,independent spaces, and cyclic ﬂats. Our primary viewpoint\n\nwill be through the rank function.\n\nNotation 2.1. We denote by M= (E, ρ)a q-matroid on the ambient space E with rank function ρ. We\n\ndenote the set of independent spaces (those X≤E such that ρ(X) = dim(X)) of M by I(M).\n\nThe theory of cyclic ﬂats for q-matroids was formalised in [1]. We recall the deﬁnition here and establish\n\nnotation.\n\nDeﬁnition 2.2. For a q-matroid M= (E, ρ), we say that X≤E is a ﬂat if ρ(X)< ρ(X⊕ ⟨v⟩) for all\n\nv∈E\\X. We say that X is cyclic if ρ(H)=ρ(X) for all H≤X such that dim(H) = dim(X)−1.\n\nNotation 2.3. For a q-matroid M, we denote its set of ﬂats by F(M) and its set of cyclic spaces by C(M).\n\nThe set of cyclic ﬂats is F(M)∩C(M), which we denote by Z(M). We may use the same established notation\n\nI,F,C, and Z analogously for matroids.\n\nIt was established in [1,11] that a q-matroid is uniquely determined not only by its rank function, but\n\nalternatively by its independent spaces, ﬂats, cyclic spaces, or cyclic ﬂats together with the rank function\n\n2\n\nrestricted to the cyclic ﬂats. Therefore, a q-matroid may equivalently be deﬁned using any one of these\n\nnotions.\n\nThe following standard deﬁnitions from q-matroid theory will be convenient for our purposes.\n\nDeﬁnition 2.4. For a q-matroid M= (E, ρ) and X≤E, an inclusion-maximal element of I(M) that is\n\ndirect sum of M 1 and M 2 is the q-matroid M 1⊕M 2= (E 1⊕E 2, ρ), where for each X≤E 1⊕E 2,\n\nρ(X) = min{ρ 1(π 1(Y)) + ρ 2(π 2(Y)) + dim(X)−dim(Y) : Y≤X}.\n\nDeﬁnition 2.8. [2, Deﬁnition 20] Let M 1= (E 1, ρ 1) and M 2= (E 2, ρ 2)be q-matroids. The free product\n\nof M 1 and M 2 is the q-matroid M 1□M 2= (E 1⊕E 2, ρ), where X∈ I(M 1□M 2) if and only if\n\nπ 1(X∩(E 1⊕0)) ∈ I(M 1) and ρ 1(E 1)−ρ 1(π 1(X∩(E 1⊕0))) ≥dim(π 2(X)) −ρ 2(π 2(X)).\n\n3\n\nWe recall the following result from [9], which draws an important parallel with matroid theory.\n\nTheorem 2.9. [9, Theorem 6.2] For q-matroids M 1 and M 2,\n\nZ(M 1⊕M 2)={Z 1⊕Z 2:Z 1∈ Z(M 1) and Z 2∈ Z(M 2)}.\n\nThe important parallel that the above theorem draws with matroid theory is that if N 1 and N 2 are\n\nmatroids, then Z(N 1⊕N 2)={Z 1∪Z 2:Z 1∈ Z(N 1) and Z 2∈ Z(N 2)}. This analogy with respect to the\n\ndirect 